The Angiosperm Phylogeny Group

synopsis and therefore not based on an analysis of a speciﬁc
DNA data set. However, before moving on to changes
needed in APG IV to produce an update (APG V), we ﬁrst
revisit the two general principles under which APG has
operated.
In addition to the APG classiﬁcation of angiosperms, there
have been similar recent eﬀorts, for example, that of the
Bryophyte Phylogeny Group (BPG; Li et al., 2024) and the
Pteridophyte Phylogeny Group for lycopods and ferns
(PPG, 2016). Both, however, have been almost entirely based
on plastid DNA data, and in this respect, APG is leading the
eﬀorts to incorporate the large amount of nuclear gene data
being produced during the “genomic era.” Similar eﬀorts
have been made in several angiosperm families, for example,
the Legume Phylogeny Working Group (LPWG; 2017), the
Grass Phylogeny Working Group (GPWG, 2001, 2012, 2025),
and orchids (Chase et al., 2015), among many.

2 General Principles of
Classiﬁcation (a reprise)

the

APG

The authors of the previous APG classiﬁcations have
recognized that multiple classiﬁcations may be derived
from published evidence, and the choice of which to accept
has always depended on the author′s taxonomic philosophy
and approach. There is more than one way to cut up a
phylogenetic tree, and authors have varied opinions on how
best to do this to produce a classiﬁcation. In APG (1998), the
authors explained the process used to determine the number
of orders to recognize and the criteria that were considered
important. APG (1998) previously recognized the families and
orders that had been in the prevailing morphologically based
classiﬁcations of that time to provide stability and familiarity,
but these did not always have the traditional composition.
This task was made easier because in the 1970s, substantial
agreement had developed among the systems being
proposed. However, the authors of APG (1998) made a
conscious eﬀort to minimize the number of orders to keep
the system as simple as possible. Thus, the general
arrangements of the monocot orders and families were
those proposed by Dahlgren et al. (1985). The original APG
(1998) for all angiosperms was similar in structure to that
proposed by Dahlgren (1975), which was notable in the
use of morphological cladistics to provide a picture of
angiosperm relationships. Dahlgren (1975) recognized
roughly 30 superorders, which is similar in number and
names to the 40 orders in APG (1998). This general structure
has been ma

=== page 7 ===
plastid versus nuclear incongruence at various levels across
angiosperms. In addition to the discordance between
organellar and nuclear trees, studies have also highlighted
conﬂicting relationships identiﬁed with multiple nuclear
genes (e.g., Escobar et al., 2011; Som, 2015; Sun et al., 2015).
Although noise, error, and paralogy probably contribute to
some discordance between genes in the nuclear genomes,
there has also been increasing support for biological causes
of incongruence, including ILS, gene ﬂow via hybridization/
introgression, extensive rate heterogeneity, and possibly
others. Genomic data have revealed multiple instances of
deep incongruence in the angiosperms (Stull et al., 2023;
Zuntini et al., 2024), although few of these phenomena aﬀect
the classiﬁcation of families and orders, which are the focus
of APG. The position of Ericales has been unresolved due to
them likely being a product of ancient hybridization between
ancestors of Cornales and the larger asterid clade (c. 120 Ma;
Stull et al., 2020). Likewise, Guo et al. (2021) found evidence
that ancient hybridization could account for the incongruent
phylogenetic placement of Chloranthales and magnoliids in
nuclear and plastid analyses (Table 1).
3.5 Plastid and nuclear trees
In APG V, we base alterations of APG IV (2016) on wellsupported nuclear results, particularly if also supported by
large analyses of plastome data (Li et al., 2021). The results
on which circumscription of APG V orders and families are
based have been generally consistent across phylogenetic
methods and assumptions, and are parallel in coalescent,
concatenated (combined), and most forms of weighted
analyses. If extensive nuclear gene‐tree incongruence has
been detected, such that the position of a group is
unresolved or only weakly supported, then we indicate
uncertainty of result by considering them as “unplaced” to
order or major clade (e.g., fabids, asterids). Likewise, if a set

851

of relationships varies depending on the method used, we
also indicate this as a polytomy for the groups concerned,
unless noted in the section below on speciﬁc problematic
parts of APG V (e.g., placement of Dasypogonaceae in
Arecales, which has considerable gene‐tree discordance at
that node, that is, no portion of identical gene trees greater
than 50% or, out of three possible quartet topologies, no
more than two above one‐third). Positions of some taxa in
some plastid analyses should simply be discarded if they
conﬂict with well‐su

